  {
    "episode_id": 86213,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86213.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, it says that the answers aren't as easy to find as they should be. Now, a lot of that, I mean, I think on the mental health side, it was probably only about 10 or 12 therapists, psychiatrists, psychologists that I saw. And the bulk of that number comes from physical therapists, comes from physical therapists, massage therapists, osteopathic surgeons, chronic pain specialists, personal trainers, an acupuncturist, couple podiatrists, basically desperate effort for what's going on here. And I think one of the biggest things it says is healthcare is very specialized. You don't really have that person who goes, OK, let's look at the whole body and mind and get the diagnosis right first. It's very fragmented. And the whole thing is connected. And that's almost a cliche at this point. But just because your foot hurts, it may not have anything directly to do with your foot, it may be connected to something else in your body. You know, anxiety and depression can also lead to chronic pain. It is all connected and finding those connections is very difficult.",
