  {
    "episode_id": 86213,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86213.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, no, it's a weird thing. And this is, you know, it's funny people think the world we live in today and all the conflicts we have, but the depth of the sexism in America is really not fully understood because up until probably the mid-70s, the expectation was if you were a woman, and you got married, you didn't need a job. In fact, if you had a job, you were taking it from a man who had a family to raise. The State Department didn't allow married women to work in certain posts, and they certainly didn't allow a woman who had a child out of wedlock. She couldn't keep me into a job, basically. So that forced the decision to some extent. And what I can gather from talking to her in the situation It was an agonizing decision. I mean, right up to the moment of me hopping on that plane, you know, she wasn't sure that it was what she wanted to do.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
