  {
    "episode_id": 86213,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86213.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I was the basically the cliche first of my family to go to college. My father was a ramp serviceman, a baggage handler for nine airlines. My mother basically raised me and my two brothers who were not adopted by the way. You know, my father had big ambitions for me. And that was the other thing that sort of drove my childhood. And I think in retrospect, you know, he felt a responsibility. Like he really liked his younger sister. He's taken on this responsibility. He's got to do something with it. So, he pushed me, you know, to do more than I might otherwise would have. He expected big things with me. And I was like, you know, I don't know, mediocre. It's like, you know, I was an okay athlete. I was like, you know, I had like a 3-2 in high school. I was okay. But it wasn't like I was the guy who was like, oh, that guy's going to be a star, you know, whatever. But I had that feeling, I got to do something. And I felt like I had to challenge myself, which is what led me to go to Fordham University in New York. It's complicated. I had a friend who was from New York who was family moved to CTAC when we were in high school, and we became friends. And he went to Fordham, and I followed him there.",
