    "turn_text": "Yes and no, that lesson primarily came into play as I was pursuing when I wanted out of life. It drove me to be willing to, this is hard, this is difficult, but I'm just gonna do it. The problem when the anxiety and the pain hit is, as I document, I had no idea what to do. I mean, for the most part, when I was trying to accomplish something in life, and I felt like, I can't do it. I can't do it. I would like, oh, God, I was very self-pity. But then I'd be like, OK, what are you going to do? All right, you can sit there on your wall all day and go to work. So I had my little lists, my little yellow notepads, as far as writing down. What am I going to do? What am I going to do? Or when I was running for the state senate, I could doorbell. I could walk out the door and I could go knock on doors. I could do something. Here, What can I do? I have this feeling of unbelievable anxiety. I can't sleep. I can barely eat. I can't go away. I'm in pain. I'm trying to exercise, but I can't. I was lost, basically. So I would say no, actually. Well, I guess in one sense, I never gave up. Okay, that's why I went over a hundred different people. I'm not going to stop until either I just physically can't move forward or until I find the answer. So in that sense it was helpful.",
