  {
    "episode_id": 86213,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86213.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "No. they should have, but they didn't. So I won that election, and like I said, it was very confident. Now my mother passed my adoptive mother, and the only mother really, my mother is the way I think of it, passed away, literally the night before I got elected the state senate. And my father passed away. Home alone living in the house that I grew up in Yeah, but I was in the Senate life was good in a whole wide variety of ways But then just this and crud it was depression. It was like nothing interested me. I just had this black feeling for like three or four months that I told nobody about a story I don't put in the book, actually a colleague of mine who I had interned for when I was in law school called me one day. He was a state house member. I was in the Senate. He called me, said, Adam, are you okay? And I was like, I'm fine. I walk past you today and you just look terrible. It didn't even occur to me that I was walking around looking like. But it went away and I just didn't really give it much thought. And whenever I felt the 1994 story is when I failed to pass a bill and I was supposed to pass a long complicated story and I broke down in tears trying to explain it to the caucuses. But that feeling of failure just It just broke me for the moment.",
