  {
    "episode_id": 86213,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86213.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 75,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, I feel like a better way to put it. I loved politics. I loved being involved, but States Senate was a part-time job. I was working as a part-time prosecutor, and I was looking for a full-time job in politics. I'll be perfectly honest about it. And the Republican Revolution was in 1994. Actually, the state of Washington got hammered with that. We had eight Democrats out of the nine seats in 94. We lost six of them. So it was 7-2. We probably lost six seats in the state. So I ran for one of those. It was a very stressful experience. I don't think I'd tell the story in the book. I'm trying to make sure I don't have a primary bunch of different things. Then there was actually Judith Billings, who was the superintendent of public construction. I'd worked with a good person and everything. I started hearing this rumor that she's got AIDS and she's going to run for the 9th District seat. I'm like, but turned out true. She did have its transfusion issue, I think. I forget what it was, but then she's still doing well, which was great. But I went through this period when she became like this huge national story and everybody thought she was going to run. And in the middle of all this, the DCCC asked me to come back to DC to, you know, do a candidate thing, which I didn't want to do because my district, all right? I ain't getting any votes in DC, okay? I'm knocking on doors, I'm staying in the district, but I did it. And then the planes delayed and I'm stuck in Detroit in like January or something, trying to connect. And there's Judith Billing, some of the national news, and I'm like, are you kidding me? And literally, I just put my head down the counter. The exact thing I said is, why did I do this to myself? I didn't have to do this. I could have done something a lot more peaceful, a lot more stressful. And I probably spent an hour like that. And then I got out my notepad and said, okay, what are we going to do?",
