  {
    "episode_id": 86213,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86213.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 106,
    "turn_text": "Well, actually, if you're going to come in as a freshman, coming into the minority is not the worst situation because it's a high pressure situation. You're trying to figure out, okay, how do I make all this work? Everything you describe. How do you make it work for your family? And if you're in the majority, there's a lot more pressure. Now, after 10 years, you start to think, okay, is this ever going to change? But a lot of the job of Congress doesn't mean to do with whether or not you're in the majority of the minority. You're serving your constituents. You're responding to emails. You're dealing with Social Security, Medicare, tax problems. You're talking about issues and introducing bills. It's not terrible being in the minority, in my view, because a lot of what I do is about the constituents, how are we representing them, how am I listening to them and responding to them in a consistent basis. But it's different.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Coming into the minority as a freshman is not the worst situation.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The core responsibilities of Congress involve serving constituents, responding to communications, addressing policy areas like Social Security and taxes, and introducing legislation.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Being in the minority is not a terrible position.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Being in the minority creates a high-pressure environment where one must figure out how to make things work.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "A significant part of congressional work focuses on constituent representation and responsiveness.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "When in the majority, there is significantly more pressure compared to being in the minority.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "After 10 years, members may begin to question whether change will ever occur.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Pressure levels are perceived differently depending on one's status as majority or minority member.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Constituent engagement and responsiveness are central measures of effective representation.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Long-term service in Congress leads to disillusionment about systemic change.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The value of being in the minority is judged by its impact on constituent service rather than partisan power.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Effective governance involves consistent, ongoing interaction with constituents beyond symbolic or legislative roles.",
        "confidence": 0.87
