    "turn_text": "surplus for four years. And the Republicans were in charge of the House, and they didn't like to be in session that much. I mean, we used to have a Tuesday through Thursday schedule. I mean, when the Republicans were in the majority in early 2000s, you know, we'd fly out Tuesday morning have votes after 6 30 Wednesday Thursday. We were done by two and flying back. So, you know, we wasn't necessarily, it was a calmer time, but then you'd have to write a whole other book to describe what happened. But I think information, more people became involved in politics. It became democratized. You can go on the internet, you can go on social media. A lot more people got involved, a lot more people got intense about it. The Tea Party emerged after Barack Obama was elected president. We're engaged in two wars. The Great Recession hit in 2009. I remember standing on the floor when that first bailout bill went down and everybody's on their phone watching the stock market go unfortunate analogy given what's coming up next week. And then it just got a lot more intense and back and forth and you're on social media all the time and, you know, you're on cable all the time. I mean, it's just is a lot more of a 24-7 job than it was prior to 2008.",
