    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/6/86213.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 191,
    "turn_text": "and psychiatrist. The basic idea that regardless of how good you are at anything, you just have a fundamental worth as a human being. And that's where it all starts. And that's what I didn't have. And I don't know if it's because I was adopted and because I had in there a document in the book there were other challenges with my adoptive parents and the family I grew up in. But for some reason I felt like I had to prove myself every day. that I didn't have a basic worth, which is why the prospect of failure of not doing something successfully was such a huge threat to me. Because if I didn't succeed, if I wasn't everything that I felt like I had to be, then I didn't have any worth. And that's what I argued with my psychologist about. I said, well, look, He said, well, where do you think you get your self-worth? And I said, well, I'm good at my job. So you think that's where your value comes from. How would your work? And I'm like, well, no, I'm a good father. I'm a good husband. So you think your self-worth comes from your deeds. And I was like, well, duh, what else you got? Now, and the basic thing is, and this sort of weird new EEG, whatever, it's because you're alive. No matter who you are as a human being, you have a fundamental self-worth which can never be challenged regardless of what you do. And that's not to say that it doesn't matter what you do. But if you don't have that sense of self-worth, day in and day out is an existential struggle. And if that was always creeping down inside me. And then you also have to be honest about what's going on in your life, what happened in your childhood. You really can't bury this stuff. That's actually a psychologist years before this was when I was first starting to struggle, was trying to explain the concept of suppression. you know, the stuff in your life that you've suppressed. And I remember thinking, well, I'm 48 years old. I've suppressed it for this long. Could I just do it for another 30, 35 years and call it good? Now, you really can't. You know, if there's things that were stressful about your childhood, about your life right now, it is helpful to be honest with them. It doesn't necessarily have to be with a trained therapist, but certainly you have to have people around you who enable you to be honest with yourself. Okay, you know, what are you really feeling? Because that anger, that anxiety that you're feeling, you think it's because that car cut you off on the road. It's probably got a little bit more to do with relationships or things that are more fundamental that you haven't addressed. So those two things, a healthy narcissism and honesty about who you are and what's going on in your life. Crucial steps to starting to deal with anxiety and depression. What was it like getting off drugs? Oh, it was tough It was really tough, but then you know I was liberated You know, I just always do that that wasn't the answer Deep down it was just a crutch You know and certainly because I took tram at all and oxycodone you know for the pain for a long time and you know, and then the clonazapam was the big one, but also I tried a SSRI pro, I tried selexa, but you know, pro saxo loft is what that is, it's supposed to even you out. Not helpful, but by and large, and I don't want to overstate this case, I don't want to that there are undoubtedly some people in this country who need to be on medication. But we way over prescribe the stuff. And in most cases, it blocks our ability to deal with the mental issues that we are facing. I had another clip. We have about",
